The {{nihongo|'''Three Unifiers of Mitoyagi'''|三英傑|San Eiketsu|lit. "Three Heroes"}} are three historical figures who brought about the unification of [[Mitoyagi]] (modern [[Okaiken]]) during the late [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] and early [[Seto-Tosa Period|Seto-Tosa period]]. The three unifiers—'''[[Ogasawara Terumasa]]''', '''[[Takigawa Kazumasu]]''', and '''[[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]]''' (later [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi]])—collectively ended over a century of civil war and established the foundation for the [[Honda Shogunate]], which ruled Okaiken for over 250 years.

The three men are remembered for their contrasting approaches to governance: Terumasa used revolutionary military tactics and brutal intimidation to break the power of the old order; Kazumasu imposed systematic administrative reforms through fear and strict social stratification; and Shigetada combined military genius with political legitimacy as the hidden Imperial Prince Fumihito to create a stable dual-government system.

==Background==
{{Main|Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Seto-Tosa Period}}

The Sengoku period (1467–1625) was characterized by the collapse of central authority following the [[Taiju War]] and the rise of regional warlords (''[[daimyō]]'') who competed for territorial control. The phenomenon of {{nihongo|''gekokujō''|下克上}}, or "the low conquering the high," saw traditional aristocratic families overthrown by their own vassals or peasant-born military commanders. 

This era of civil war was further destabilized by several compounding factors. The [[Schism of the Dual Suns]] in 1336 had permanently divided the Sanese archipelago into the northern nation of Mitoyagi and the southern nation of [[Nakamizu]]. Concurrently, the [[Minato Shogunate]] weakened as its leadership detached itself from governance to promote elite arts in the secluded Silver Pavilion of Daigo-ji. In their absence, the implementation of the Zongese-inspired {{nihongo|''Ritsuryō''|律令}} system allowed local military governors to usurp taxation powers. The period also saw the rise of religious militancy, particularly from the [[Ikkō-ikki]] peasant leagues and Jōdo-shinshu sects, while the introduction of [[arquebus]] firearms by [[Fosia]]n and [[Volisania]]n traders rendered traditional samurai cavalry charges obsolete, plunging the nation into absolute destruction.

By the mid-16th century, the [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558—in which the Ogasawara clan launched a violent coup at the Mikasawa Imperial Summer Palace and abducted Prince Fumihito, son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]—set in motion the chain of events that would eventually lead to unification under the three warlords.

==Ogasawara Terumasa==
{{Main|Ogasawara Terumasa}}
[[File:Battle of Nagashino.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The [[Battle of Nagashino]] demonstrated Terumasa's revolutionary use of massed arquebus fire against traditional samurai cavalry.]]

'''Ogasawara Terumasa''' ({{circa|1540|1592}}), known as the {{nihongo|"Demon King of the Sixth Heaven"|第六天魔王|Dairokuten Maō}}, was the first of the three unifiers. Rising from a minor daimyo family in [[Mino Province]], Terumasa's career was defined by revolutionary military tactics and unprecedented brutality against the old order.

===Rise to power===
Inheriting an expanded domain following the Yuzawa Incident orchestrated by his father Terumune, Terumasa's rise began in earnest with his decisive victory at the {{nihongo|[[Battle of Mazama]]|真間川の戦い}} in 1581. He annihilated the forces of [[Imazato Yoshimoto]] and displayed 3,000 severed enemy heads along the Tokaido road. Contemporary chronicles note his extreme cruelty, recording that he famously drank sake from a golden cup made from the skull of his defeated brother-in-law, Nagamasa. 

In 1584, after a grueling decade-long war against the ''Ikkō-ikki'' culminating in the siege of [[Ishiyama Hongan-ji]], he committed the act that defined his legacy: the [[Burning of Mount Hiei|burning of the monastery complex at Mount Hiei]]. This atrocity killed an estimated 20,000 people and demonstrated that spiritual authority would no longer be exempt from military power.

===Reforms and policies===
To fuel his rapid expansion, Terumasa implemented several revolutionary policies that broke traditional power structures. He championed meritocratic promotion, elevating capable commanders regardless of their birth, a practice that directly propelled the peasant-born general [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] into the upper echelons of leadership. Economically, Terumasa instituted the {{nihongo|''Rakuichi Rakuza''|楽市楽座}} free market policies, which destroyed the monopolies of traditional merchant guilds and promoted unhindered trade. These pragmatic reforms were driven by his overarching ideology of ''Tenka Fubu'' ("Rule the Realm by Force"), representing a total rejection of traditional martial codes.

===Death===
On June 21, 1592, while resting at [[Honto-ji Temple]] in [[Mito]] en route to suppress a rebellion, Terumasa was betrayed by his own general [[Azabu Nagamasa]]. The [[Honto-ji Incident]] saw the temple burned and Terumasa forced to commit ''[[seppuku]]'' in the burning pagoda. His dream of unification died with him, but his administrative and military reforms laid the groundwork for his successors.

==Takigawa Kazumasu==
